{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1784881826,"nonce":"lGuIL5txKfTtQR3rk6tSnN0fWa8=","target":"b48df22940a3279ad6c5915caa8390d9e9645d5dc9e24ad5680f587322e06a59","message":"**Parent:** ADR-0060 — Org settings surface (`docs/adr/0060-org-settings-connections-and-secret-management.md`).\n\n**What to build**\n\nThe payoff slice: make Forgejo **actually onboardable**. Add repo→Project **binding** and per-repo **webhook registration** to a Forgejo connection. In the codebase a Project *is* a `forge_repos` binding (there is no `projects` table), so binding a repo is exactly what brings a Project into existence and makes it appear on the Repos page.\n\nExpose `bind_repo`/`unbind_repo` and `register_webhook` (ADR-0046 — a real capability for Forgejo, a no-op for GitHub) behind the connections API, plus UI on a Forgejo connection to add/remove repos and register their webhooks. Once bound and webhooked, a push to that Forgejo repo normalizes to an Event and triggers a Run — the end-to-end path GitHub already has. GitHub binding stays auto (untouched); manual bind is the Forgejo path.\n\n**Acceptance criteria**\n\n- [ ] Endpoints to bind/unbind a repo on a connection (`Administer`-gated); binding creates the Project (`forge_repos` row) and it appears via `GET /v1/repos`.\n- [ ] `register_webhook` is invoked for a bound Forgejo repo (adapter capability), and the resulting delivery is accepted by the existing `/webhooks/forgejo` handler.\n- [ ] UI on a Forgejo connection lists bound repos and supports add/remove + register-webhook.\n- [ ] Unbinding removes the Project from the Repos list.\n- [ ] Functional test (Forgejo fake + Postgres): bind → Project visible → simulated webhook delivery → Run created.\n- [ ] Live-Forgejo push→run verification is tracked as a follow-up (needs a real instance + tunnel, like the helm-dogfood E2E) — not a blocker for this slice.\n\n**Blocked by:** 8b3cf79 (Slice 4: Forgejo connection create).","files":null}]}